DAY 18 – LIST COMPREHENSION AND GENERATORS

1. LIST

A list is a collection of items.

Lists are written using square brackets [].

A list can contain different types of data.


2. LIST COMPREHENSION

List comprehension is a short and powerful way to create new lists by using a loop in one line.

Syntax:

[expression for item in iterable if condition]

expression → What to do with the item.
item → Variable name for each value.
iterable → Collection being looped over.
condition → Optional condition used to filter items.


3. LIST COMPREHENSION WITH DIFFERENT DATA TYPES

List comprehension can be used with:

• Strings
• Integers
• Floats
• Boolean values
• Tuples
• Dictionaries


4. NESTED LIST COMPREHENSION

Nested list comprehension contains more than one loop in a single list comprehension.

It can be used to work with nested data and flatten nested collections.


5. GENERATORS

Generators are a special type of iterable.

They allow data to be processed efficiently without storing everything in memory.

Generators generate values lazily using the yield keyword.


6. WHY DO WE NEED GENERATORS?

Memory Efficiency:
Generators generate values on the fly instead of storing the entire dataset.

Performance Boost:
They avoid unnecessary storage and can improve performance for large datasets.

Infinite Sequences:
Generators can work with infinite sequences without using large amounts of memory.

Pipeline Processing:
They are useful when data is processed in chunks, such as streaming data.


7. HOW GENERATORS WORK

A generator function looks like a normal function but uses yield instead of return.

When a generator function is called, it does not execute immediately.

It returns a generator object.

The generator can be iterated using a loop or the next() function.


8. YIELD KEYWORD

The yield keyword produces a value without terminating the function.

The function pauses at yield and remembers its state.

When next() is called again, execution continues from where it stopped.


9. NEXT() FUNCTION

The next() function is used to manually iterate through generator values.

It moves the execution to the next yield statement.

When all values are exhausted, StopIteration is raised.


10. FUNCTIONS VS GENERATORS

Functions:

• Store all values in memory.
• Run once and return a value.
• Use return to return a result.
• Cannot be directly iterated over.

Generators:

• Generate values one by one.
• Save memory.
• Can pause and resume execution.
• Use yield to produce multiple values.
• Can be used in loops like an iterator.


11. REAL-WORLD APPLICATIONS OF GENERATORS

• Streaming large data
• Infinite news feeds
• Log file processing
• Music streaming


KEY TAKEAWAY

List Comprehension provides a concise way to create lists.

Generators produce values one at a time and are memory-efficient.

yield produces a value and pauses execution.

next() moves the generator to the next value.